# TypeScript code style

Optimize every change for the next reader. Prefer the boring solution that is obviously correct over the clever one that needs explaining.

## Default posture

- Solve only the problem in front of you. Do not add configuration, hooks, or extension points for hypothetical future needs.
- Prefer plain functions and small modules. Introduce a class only for real state or a required framework contract.
- Reach for the standard library and existing repo helpers before a new dependency or abstraction.
- Duplicate a few lines rather than abstract over two dissimilar call sites. Wait for the third real case.
- Delete dead code, unused exports, and commented-out blocks instead of leaving them.

## Structure and flow

- Keep functions short and single-purpose: under 40 lines and doing one thing the name fully describes.
- Return early with guard clauses; do not nest the happy path.
- Keep nesting to three levels or less. Extract a named helper when you need a fourth.
- Prefer pure functions that take inputs and return values. Isolate I/O, network, and mutable state at the edges.
- Never mutate an argument the caller still owns unless the name makes that explicit (`sortInPlace`, `append`).
- Put all imports at the top of the module. No mid-file imports and no dynamic `import()` inside functions unless required to break a documented circular dependency.
- Prefer `import type { … }` for type-only imports.

```typescript
// Prefer
type Status =
  | { kind: "ok"; value: number }
  | { kind: "err"; message: string }
  | { kind: "pending" };

export function formatStatus(status: Status): string {
  switch (status.kind) {
    case "ok":
      return String(status.value);
    case "err":
      return status.message;
    case "pending":
      return "pending";
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = status;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}

// Avoid
export function formatStatus(input: any): string {
  const s = input as { kind?: string; value?: number; message?: string };
  if (s.kind === "ok") {
    if (s.value != null) return String(s.value);
    else return "";
  } else {
    return s.message ?? "unknown";
  }
}
```

## Naming

- Use `camelCase` for values and functions, `PascalCase` for types/classes/components, `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for true constants.
- Name after intent and domain meaning: `retryCount`, not `num`; `activeUsers`, not `list2`.
- Use verb phrases for functions (`loadConfig`, `isExpired`) and nouns for values and types.
- Avoid inventing abbreviations; reserve one-letter names for conventional tight scopes (`i`, `x`, `_`).

## Types

- Annotate exported function parameters and return types. Let locals infer when the type is obvious.
- Prefer `unknown` over `any`. Narrow with `typeof`, `in`, discriminated unions, or type guards — not `as` casts.
- Avoid `any`, `as any`, `as unknown as T`, `@ts-ignore`, and bare `@ts-expect-error`. If you must suppress, use `@ts-expect-error` with a one-line reason.
- Model variants with discriminated unions (`kind` / `type` fields). Prefer string-literal unions over most `enum`s.
- Use `readonly` / `Readonly<T>` / `ReadonlyArray<T>` for data that should not change.
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes that may be extended; prefer `type` for unions, intersections, and mapped types.
- Never use a mutable shared default: do not write `config: Config = DEFAULT_CONFIG` when callers can mutate it. Use `config?: Config` and fall back inside, or freeze the default.

## Exhaustiveness

- In `switch` statements over discriminated unions or enums, handle every variant and end with a `never` check in `default` so new variants fail at compile time.
- Do not use a catch-all `default` that silently returns a fallback for union cases that should be exhaustive.

## Errors and async

- Validate inputs and fail fast with a specific `Error` (or domain error class) whose message names the offending value.
- Prefer throwing or returning a typed `Result` over sentinel `null`/`undefined` for unexpected failure. Reserve `null` for genuinely absent optional values.
- Keep `try` blocks small. Catch the narrowest failure you can handle; never swallow with an empty `catch`.
- Use `async`/`await` instead of raw `Promise.then` chains. Do not mix styles in one flow.
- Never log or embed secrets, tokens, or credentials.

## Comments and docs

- Comment only what the code cannot say: a non-obvious constraint, trade-off, or workaround and its reason.
- Do not narrate the next line or leave change-log comments (`// fixed bug`, `// new code`).
- Exported public APIs that are non-obvious get a short JSDoc stating what they do and what they throw or return.

## Tests

- Cover observable behavior, edge cases, and the error paths you raise.
- One behavior per test, with a name that states the expectation.
- Arrange-act-assert; assert on outputs and side effects, not private internals.
- Keep unit tests fast and deterministic; mock network, filesystem, and time at the boundaries.

## Tooling

- Match the repository's formatter and linter (typically Prettier/ESLint, Biome, or oxlint) instead of hand-formatting.
- Keep `strict` TypeScript options on. Do not weaken `tsconfig` to silence errors.
- Run the type checker, formatter, linter, and relevant tests before handing work off.

## Avoid

`any`-driven designs, non-exhaustive switches, inline or mid-file imports, deep `Partial`/`as` cast chains, mutable shared defaults, mutating caller-owned inputs, nested ternaries that pack several ideas into one expression, single-use class hierarchies, and `Object.assign` / spread soup that hides required fields. Each one trades a few keystrokes for real reader effort.
